Linux Authentication Log Analyzer
Prerequisites
Basic Linux command line knowledge (cd, ls, cat)
Understanding of text files and file paths
Familiarity with basic text searching concepts
SSH access to a Linux machine
Learning Objectives
By completing this lab, you will:

Understand Linux authentication log structure and location
Parse and analyze auth.log files for security events
Detect failed login attempts and patterns
Identify successful authentication events
Recognize suspicious activity indicators
Create basic security monitoring scripts
Environment Setup
Step 1: Access Your Lab Machine
Click the Start Lab button to provision your Linux cloud machine. Once ready, connect via SSH using the provided credentials.

Step 2: Verify Log Access
Check that authentication logs are accessible:

sudo ls -lh /var/log/auth.log*
You should see auth.log and possibly rotated logs (auth.log.1, etc.).

Step 3: Install Required Tools
Most tools are pre-installed, but verify:

which grep awk sed
All three commands should return paths. These are standard on Linux systems.

Step 4: Create Working Directory
mkdir ~/log-analysis
cd ~/log-analysis
Task 1: Understanding Authentication Logs
Step 1: Examine Log Structure
View recent authentication events:

sudo tail -n 20 /var/log/auth.log
Key log components:

Timestamp (e.g., Dec 15 10:23:45)
Hostname
Service (sshd, sudo, login, etc.)
Process ID [PID]
Event message
Step 2: Generate Test Authentication Events
Create some login activity for analysis:

# This will fail intentionally - creates a failed login entry
su - nonexistentuser

# Check sudo access - creates successful sudo entry
sudo whoami
Step 3: View Your Generated Events
sudo tail -n 5 /var/log/auth.log
You should see the events you just created.

Task 2: Detecting Failed Login Attempts
Step 1: Search for Failed Password Attempts
sudo grep "Failed password" /var/log/auth.log
This shows all failed password attempts with usernames and source IPs.

Step 2: Count Failed Attempts by User
sudo grep "Failed password" /var/log/auth.log | awk '{print $(NF-5)}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn
Command breakdown:

grep: Finds failed password lines
awk '{print $(NF-5)}': Extracts username (5th field from end)
sort | uniq -c: Counts occurrences
sort -rn: Sorts by count (highest first)
Step 3: Identify Source IPs of Failed Attempts
sudo grep "Failed password" /var/log/auth.log | awk '{print $(NF-3)}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn
This shows which IP addresses have the most failed attempts.

